Notebook[{
Cell["Hypermatrix", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"78609705-9fbb-1441-bfe5-9d8201f4e38d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "934091cf-56a6-a847-a5b4-d8cb7f1c55f5"],
 " ",
 "represents the given arrays as a hypermatrix, holding them in canonical \
order."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"9607751e-dac4-4e4f-b882-370daba207d1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"Hypermatrix", "[", 
    RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "]"}], 
   "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5894898a-beae-134d-9bc5-d3de3176c519"],
 " ",
 "is equivalent."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"f2667668-994a-e74f-8713-9b63b8bb7578"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"58af1760-efb8-ce4b-88b9-3a7b9e411f18"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays are ordered by array order, lowest first; then by dimensions, \
shorter first, compared term by term; then by symmetry, taking \"Symmetric\" \
before \"Cyclic\" before \"Rigid\".\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"1e40b959-b021-9346-ad8c-2955484300ae"],

Cell["\<\
Two arrays alike in all three are put in the Wolfram Language's own canonical \
order, so that a hypermatrix is a function of the set of arrays it was given \
and of nothing else. Giving the same arrays in a different order produces the \
identical expression.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7ec76bb8-2922-f349-bca8-2631e82b77c3"],

Cell["\<\
An irregular array is necessarily \"Rigid\", since only a regular array can \
carry a symmetry, so the symmetry criterion separates the regular arrays \
among themselves.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"ef3d0376-95a0-4445-8373-658754d39b9e"],

Cell["\<\
Arrays of any kind of entry sit together freely. Raw entries given in place \
of an array are promoted to an ArrayObject.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"156b6df3-8b7a-1a4d-bcbc-3338d6fa95fd"],

Cell["\<\
Length gives the number of arrays, and Normal the entries of each of them in \
order.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"0ff9bee6-8147-1d4e-bbef-c84c3cedd41c"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"e68dfc07-b391-c844-8d13-c94d367feab8"],

Cell["The arrays are sorted whatever order they are given in:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"f7a86c5d-85d3-0647-9f6d-cb804ca20ac4"],

Cell["\<\
hm = Hypermatrix[{
   ArrayObject[Array[1 &, {2, 2, 2}], \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2}],
   ArrayObject[{1, 2, 3}],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Symmetric\"],
   ArrayObject[{{1, 2}, {2, 1}}, \"Cyclic\"]}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"25053d18-7aba-1945-a2c1-3c0fa37b4fdb"],

